WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.638
Safe request construction starts with knowing the approved purpose of the
call.

2
00:00:04.638 --> 00:00:11.731
The method, URL, query parameters, headers, body, content type, and
credentials should all match a specific API contract.

3
00:00:11.731 --> 00:00:17.119
A request that sends every available field for convenience may leak more
data than necessary.

4
00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:25.167
A request that accepts user-controlled headers or body fields without
validation may change how a remote system interprets the operation.

5
00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:31.578
URLs and headers should be built with structured APIs and controlled
values instead of unsafe string assembly.

6
00:00:31.578 --> 00:00:35.739
Query parameters should be encoded as parameters, not pasted into text.

7
00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:41.127
Header names and values should come from known configuration or carefully
validated inputs.

8
00:00:41.127 --> 00:00:45.969
Request bodies should have explicit shapes, size expectations, and content
types.

9
00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:51.835
JSON, form data, and file uploads each need clear handling rules based on
the remote service contract.

10
00:00:51.835 --> 00:00:56.473
Credentials should usually not be placed in URLs because URLs are copied
widely.

11
00:00:56.473 --> 00:01:06.021
They may appear in logs, monitoring tools, reverse proxies, referrers,
browser history in some workflows, support artifacts, exception messages,
or screenshots.

12
00:01:06.021 --> 00:01:16.456
Secrets belong in controlled authentication mechanisms appropriate for the
API, and logs should redact tokens, keys, signed values, session
identifiers, and sensitive headers.

13
00:01:16.456 --> 00:01:21.504
The request should carry only the credentials and data needed for the
approved purpose.

14
00:01:21.504 --> 00:01:24.368
Validation is still important for outbound data.

15
00:01:24.368 --> 00:01:31.871
Before sending request fields, the application should verify type, shape,
length, format, allowed values, and business intent.

16
00:01:31.871 --> 00:01:36.372
A remote API is not a substitute for local authorization or data
minimization.

17
00:01:36.372 --> 00:01:47.080
If a user is not allowed to trigger a transfer, send a file, look up a
resource, or call a partner operation, the Python client should never
construct the outbound request in the first place.
